@startuml proto-ds18b20-timing
' ===========================================================================
' proto-ds18b20-timing.puml - (c) 1-Wire slot timing, in the numbers both
' sides actually produce.
'
' Measured by tb/tb_pe_soc_ds18b20.v on real RTL (60 MHz, Icarus, the real
' SRAM macro) with firmware/ds18b20.pe:
'
'   reset pulse 485.7 us (min 480)     presence pulse 120.0 us (60..240)
'   write slots: 1-low 5.0 us           0-low 64.8 us
'   commands cc / be                    16 read slots, bytes 2b 01
'   read initiation pulses 6.2-6.3 us (1..15)
'   sample instant: 10.8 us after the response, 19.2 us before the hold ends
' ===========================================================================

title DS18B20 - 1-Wire slot timing\n<font size=12>reset 485.7 us; presence 120.0 us; write 1-low 5.0 / 0-low 64.8 us; sample +10.8 us into a 30 us window</font>

hide stereotype
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequence {
  ArrowColor #33555
  LifeLineBorderColor #33555
  LifeLineBackgroundColor #FAFAFA
  ParticipantBorderColor #33555
  ParticipantBackgroundColor #F4F7FB
  ParticipantFontName monospaced
  ActorFontName monospaced
  NoteFontName monospaced
  GroupFontName monospaced
}

participant "CPU\npe_cpu.v" as CPU
participant "pad 6\nTXPIN / PINOE" as PAD
participant "the wire\n+ pull-up" as WIRE
participant "DS18B20\nmodeled from the\ndatasheet" as SENSE
participant "the sample" as SMP

== 1. reset: COUNTED, because nothing announces it ==

CPU -> PAD : TXPIN = 0x00 - the line goes LOW
note over CPU
  COARSE pair (n2,n3) = (4,40), OW_RST = 58
  one outer step = 10 + 3*167 = <b>511 clocks = 8.5 us</b>

  total = (n1-1)*(10 + (n2-1)*(4*n3+7)) + 4
  (58-1)*511 + 4 = 29 131 clocks = <b>485.5 us</b>

  <b>AND THIS IS A COUNTED-DELAY CONSTANT, NOT A
  MICROSECOND.</b> Writing 480 straight into the outer
  counter comes out as 1 900 us, because 480 wraps to 224
  and the routine counts PASSES, not microseconds. That
  is the mistake this block exists to prevent, and it is
  why OW_RST is a peasm CONST and not a literal.
end note
note over WIRE
  <b>485.7 us of LOW</b>   (floor: 480 us)
end note
CPU -> PAD : PINOE = 0x00 - RELEASE
note over CPU
  FINE pair (2,13) from here on: one outer step is
  10 + 1*59 = <b>69 clocks = 1.15 us</b>, which is what a
  5 us slot needs. OW_T15 = 14, a 15 us settle.
end note

== 2. presence: WAITED FOR, twice, with no counted delay between ==

SENSE -> WIRE : pull LOW
note over SENSE
  <b>120.0 us</b>   (band: 60 .. 240 us)
end note
CPU -> SMP : spin until the line is LOW
CPU -> SMP : spin until the line is HIGH
note over CPU
  TWO spin loops and NO counted 15 us between them.
  The pulse's length is the sensor's to choose and the
  host cannot know it, so both edges are waited for.

  If no sensor is there the first loop simply never
  exits - correct behaviour for a bus with no timeout
  in this ISA. The TB tells "no sensor" from "sensor
  present" by whether the program got past this point.
end note
note over PAD
  The TB asserts the host's <b>pin_oe is RELEASED on every clock</b> of the
  presence pulse, not merely that the pulse is in band.
  A host still driving would fight the sensor's low, and
  the line would look plausible for a sensor that
  answered weakly.
end note
SENSE -> WIRE : release

== 3. a WRITE slot: 0xCC, then 0xBE, LSB first ==

group a 1 - short low, then release
  CPU -> PAD : PINOE = OW_DATA, TXPIN = 0x00
  note over PAD
    <b>THE WRITE PATH MUST CLAIM THE PIN.</b>
    1-Wire writes DRIVE: release-then-set-the-level
    does not pull the line down, and the pin is RELEASED
    at ph0, so TXPIN = 0 alone would leave the line HIGH
    and the sensor would see a 1 for every bit.

    The MODEL caught this: it decoded 0 write bits
    because the line never went low.
  end note
  note over WIRE
    <b>5.0 us of LOW</b>   (datasheet t0: 1 .. 15 us)
    OW_T5 = 5
  end note
  CPU -> PAD : PINOE = 0x00 - release
  note over WIRE
    <b>~55 us of HIGH</b> - the rest of the slot
    OW_T55 = 49
  end note
end group

group a 0 - low for the WHOLE slot
  CPU -> PAD : PINOE = OW_DATA, TXPIN = 0x00, and it STAYS
  note over WIRE
    <b>64.8 us of LOW</b>   (datasheet: ~60 us)
    OW_T65 = 57

    The phase goes to 3 here, not to 2: a 0's slot is
    over when the delay is, and only then is the line
    released. A previous version stored the PHASE value
    5 here as the COUNTER, which made the high period
    4.6 us and ran the slots together.
  end note
  CPU -> PAD : PINOE = 0x00 - release
end group

note over CPU, SENSE
  <b>8 write slots per byte, LSB first, and the loop needs no shift at all.</b>
  The bit to send is the byte's own bit 0: read the byte, mask bit 0, send, then
  SHR the byte so the next bit 0 is the next bit of the frame. Decoded from the
  wire: <b>0xCC (SKIP ROM) then 0xBE (READ SCRATCHPAD)</b>.
end note

== 4. a READ slot: the hybrid wait ==

group the slot, end to end
  CPU -> PAD : PINOE = OW_DATA, TXPIN = 0x00 - the HOST initiates
  note over WIRE
    <b>6.2 - 6.3 us of LOW</b> - the host's initiation pulse
    (datasheet: 1 .. 15 us)
  end note
  SENSE -> WIRE : the sensor takes the line: tRDV response
  note over SENSE
    <b>tRDV is driven at the datasheet's MAXIMUM, 15 us.</b>

    The model could have used the typical and made the
    test easier. It uses the maximum so that "the
    firmware sampled too early" is FALSIFIABLE - which is
    what makes the sample-margin check worth running at
    all.
  end note
  CPU -> PAD : PINOE = 0x00 - release
  note over PAD
    The sensor's read edges come off the <b>PADS</b>, not
    off the model's own state. That is what stopped it
    losing the master's next release while still inside
    the previous bit's hold - a bug that silently ended
    the run at 8 slots.
  end note
  CPU -> SMP : OW_T40 = 23, a fixed 25.4 us
  SMP -> WIRE : sample the LEVEL
  note over SMP
    <b>the sample: 10.8 us after the response,
    19.2 us before the hold ends</b> - inside the sensor's
    data window with margin at BOTH ends, which is what
    the TB asserts (>= 2 us at each end).

    A 0 leaves the line released for the rest of the slot;
    a 1 pulls it low again for 1-15 us. So the sample is
    a plain LEVEL read: a 0 is HIGH, a 1 is LOW.
  end note
  SENSE -> WIRE : the slot's data level, then the next initiation
end group

note over CPU, SENSE
  <b>16 read slots, and the firmware banks 2b 01.</b>
  ...and the model decodes the same 16 slots to the same two bytes. A check that
  compared the firmware against the MODEL's decode would be checking the model; it
  compares against bytes written in the TB.

  <b>THE SAMPLE IS AT A FIXED INSTANT AND NOT AT THE SENSOR'S FALLING EDGE.</b> The
  edge version was tried and it races: the line is still LOW from the host's own
  initiation pulse, so the loop exits at once and every sample lands a whole slot
  early. Counting to a fixed instant cannot race.

  That is the DHT11's lesson applied the other way round. There the host had to
  SYNCHRONISE because the sensor's bit PERIOD varies (76-78 us for a 0, 120 for a
  1). Here the host has to COUNT because the sensor's bit VALUE is a LEVEL, and a
  level read at a known instant needs no edge at all.
end note

== the slot table, in the numbers that were measured ==

note over CPU, SENSE
  |= what |= datasheet |= produced | which side | the check that holds it |=
  | reset pulse | >= 480 us | <b>485.7 us</b> | host | counted, OW_RST = 58 |
  | presence pulse | 60 .. 240 us | <b>120.0 us</b> | sensor | waited for, both edges, and pin_oe released throughout |
  | write-1 low | 1 .. 15 us | <b>5.0 us</b> | host | OW_T5 = 5; the band's own check |
  | write-0 low | ~60 us | <b>64.8 us</b> | host | OW_T65 = 57 |
  | read initiation | 1 .. 15 us | <b>6.2 - 6.3 us</b> | host | OW_T40's own pulse, band-checked |
  | sensor tRDV | 15 .. 60 us | driven at the <b>15 us MAXIMUM</b> | sensor | so "too early" is falsifiable |
  | the sample | inside the data window | <b>+10.8 us</b> in, <b>19.2 us</b> before the end | host | >= 2 us of margin at BOTH ends |
  ...
  <b>Why the sample is roughly in the middle rather than early.</b> The window opens
  15 us after the host's initiation pulse ends and closes when a 1's second pull-down
  begins. The firmware waits 25.4 us from the release, so it lands 10.8 us into a
  window that is 30 us wide - and the mutation cases ow-sample-early (2, i.e. 1.2 us
  in, still inside the sensor's 15 us response, so every bit reads as a zero) and
  ow-sample-late (45, i.e. 51.2 us, past a 0's hold) are BOTH caught by this one
  check. ow-sample-late is the interesting one: the DECODE still comes out right,
  which is the point - it is caught by the margin and by nothing else.
  ...
  <b>tLOW IS MID-BAND AND DELIBERATELY NOT WORST CASE.</b> With tLOW in 15-60 us and
  tHIGH in 1-15 us the two windows OVERLAP, and no single instant is correct for
  every legal sensor timing - the guaranteed window is one microsecond wide.
  Claiming the worst case there would be a stronger-sounding and false statement.
end note

@enduml
